course: everyday
lesson: 130
title: Birthday parties
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: An invitation needs an answer: you tell your parent and reply (RSVP) by the date, yes or no, because the host must plan food and games for the right number of children.
explain: Nobody likes being left out, so invitations are given privately or to the whole class, and you do not talk about the party in front of children who were not invited.
explain: At the party you say happy birthday to the birthday child and hello to the parents, you let them blow out their own candles, and you smile when you lose a game.
explain: The thought matters, not the price, so you never say how much a present cost; and some families and religions do not celebrate birthdays at all, which you respect.
explain: A boy in Montreal cannot go to a party because of a football match; he writes "Thank you for inviting me, I'm sorry I can't come", and his friend is not hurt.
fact: You tell your parent and reply (RSVP) to an invitation by the date, saying yes or no.
fact: The host needs to know how many children to plan for.
fact: If you cannot go, you still answer: "Thank you for inviting me, I'm sorry I can't come."
fact: Invitations are given privately or to the whole class, not in front of children who are not invited.
fact: When you arrive, you say happy birthday to the birthday child and hello to the parents.
fact: The price of a present does not matter; the thought does.
fact: If you lose a party game, you congratulate the winner and keep smiling.
fact: The birthday child blows out the candles alone, because it is their special moment.
fact: You say thank you for a party bag and when people sing happy birthday to you.
fact: You do not talk about a party in front of children who were not invited.
fact: Some religions and families do not celebrate birthdays, and that is respected.
exercise: You get a party invitation. What do you do? | Tell your parent and reply by the date, yes or no | Keep it and decide on the day | Reply only if you are going | Ask what presents there will be first, then decide whether to go
exercise: Why does the host need you to RSVP? | To know how many children to plan for | To know what present you will bring so there are no doubles | To check you have a party outfit | To send you a bill for the food
exercise: You are invited but cannot go. What do you say? | "Thank you for inviting me, I'm sorry I can't come" | Nothing, since you are not going and they will see that anyway | "Your party sounds boring anyway" | "Maybe", and then do not come
exercise: You are handing out party invitations to some of your class. How should you do it? | Give them privately, or invite the whole class | Hand them out in front of everyone so nobody can say they missed out | Read the names out loud in class | Give them to the teacher to hand out
exercise: You arrive at a birthday party. What do you do first? | Say happy birthday to the birthday child and hello to the parents | Run straight to the food table | Go off to play straight away without greeting anyone, as it is a party | Ask when the party bags are given
exercise: The birthday child opens your present. What should you not do? | Say how much it cost | Smile while they open it | Say "I hope you like it" | Wish them a happy birthday
exercise: You lose the musical chairs game. What is good party manners? | Congratulate the winner and keep smiling | Say the game was unfair and sulk | Ask to play again and again until you are the one who wins | Leave the party early
exercise: The candles on the cake are lit. Who blows them out? | The birthday child, alone | Everyone at once, to help | Whoever is closest to the cake | The youngest guest at the party
exercise: You are given a party bag as you leave. What do you say? | "Thank you" | "Is that all?" | "I wanted a bigger one" | Nothing, because you earned it
exercise: Some friends went to a party you were invited to, but others were not. Where should you talk about it? | Not in front of children who were not invited | Loudly at lunch so everyone hears how much fun it was | In class during show and tell | On the class chat for everyone
exercise: Your class sings happy birthday to you. What do you do afterwards? | Smile and say thank you | Ask them to sing it again louder | Say you do not like the song | Pretend you did not hear
exercise: Your friend's family does not celebrate birthdays. What is right? | Respect it and do not push them | Keep inviting them until they come | Tell them birthdays are compulsory | Tease them that they never get presents
test: A girl in Denver hands out invitations at the classroom door, and two children get none. Why is this unkind? | Being left out in public hurts | Invitations should always be posted by mail | The teacher must invite everyone herself | Parties must never be held on school days
test: Two weeks pass and you forgot to answer an invitation that said "RSVP by Friday". What is the problem? | The host does not know how many children to plan for | The host will cancel the whole party | Your invitation stops being valid forever | You must now bring two presents
test: At a party in Belfast, Mia wins the treasure hunt and Leo loses. What should Leo do? | Say "Well done" to Mia and keep enjoying the party | Hide the treasure better next time so that nobody can win at all | Tell Mia she cheated | Go home early because he lost
test: You gave a cheap but carefully chosen book as a present, and your friend got an expensive game from someone else. What matters most? | The thought behind the present, not the price | Which of the two presents cost more money in the shop | Who gave the biggest box | Which present arrived first
test: A boy in Sydney leans over and blows out his friend's candles first as a joke. Why is that wrong? | It is the birthday child's special moment | The cake will taste worse | Candles must only be blown out by adults | It makes the candles harder to light again
test: Your friend Hannah's family are Jehovah's Witnesses and do not celebrate birthdays. What is right? | Respect that and not push her to come to parties | Invite her again and again until she comes | Tell the class she is strange | Bring her a secret birthday cake anyway
test: On Monday at school you want to tell your friend about Saturday's party. Some classmates were not invited. What is kind? | Talk about it later, away from those classmates | Describe every game loudly in class | Show party photos to the whole table | Ask the uninvited children why they were not there
test: You cannot come to a party because you are visiting your grandma. What is the polite way to answer? | Thank them for inviting you and say you are sorry you cannot come | Do not answer at all, since you are not coming and they will work it out | Say yes and then simply not turn up | Tell them your grandma is more fun
test: got an invite, cant go tho, just ignore it? | Still reply: thank you for inviting me, I'm sorry I can't come | Ignore it, they'll guess | Throw it away | Tell them on the very day of the party, when they're getting ready
test: its my mates party, can i blow the candles with her | No, the birthday child blows them out alone | Yes, it's quicker with two | Yes, if you sing loudly | Yes, if she has a lot of candles
test: my present was cheap, its only a book, is that bad | No, the thought matters, not the price | Yes, presents must be expensive | Yes, you should buy a bigger, more expensive one instead | Only toys count as presents
test: lost musical chairs, so annoyed | Congratulate the winner and keep smiling | Say the game was unfair | Sulk until you go home | Ask to play again until you win
explain: At a birthday party in Ohio, a toddler put a burst balloon piece in his mouth and it blocked his throat; an adult had to clear it quickly.
explain: A girl in Leeds was not invited to a party and everyone talked about it at school; she felt left out for weeks, and the next year the class agreed to give invitations out of school.
fact: Pieces of burst balloons are one of the most common things young children choke on.
fact: Talking loudly about a party in front of people who are not invited can really hurt them.
test: party's over, balloon bits on the floor and lil kids around | Pick them up and bin them, kids can choke | Leave them, they're just bits of rubber and can't hurt anyone | Give them to the toddlers | Blow them up again
test: everyone at lunch talking bout my party, sam's not invited | Talk about it later, he could feel left out | Talk louder so he hears | Tell him he can't come | Show him the invitation
test: friend with nut allergy at my party, cake has nuts | Tell an adult so there's safe food for him | He can just pick the nuts out | A little bit won't hurt | Don't tell him
test: did u hear at Max's party a toddler swallowed a balloon bit n turned purple, they say he nearly died!! | Keep burst balloon bits from little kids; "nearly died" may be blown up | Balloon bits are safe to chew | Only big balloons are a choking risk, the small burst bits are too soft | Toddlers can't choke on rubber
test: apparently Zoe's mum only spent 2 dollars on Ruby's present, so cheap, everyone's talking about it | Don't spread it; the thought matters, not the price | Spread it, cheap presents are rude | Tell Ruby what it cost | Only expensive gifts count
test: my cousin says Noah's family doesn't do birthdays cos they're weird, should i tell the class | No; some families and religions don't do birthdays, and that is respected | Yes, the class should know | Invite him anyway and tease him at the party until he joins in with the cake | Ask his parents why they're weird
exam: Identify the principal reason why an invitation must be answered by the stated date. | The host needs to know how many children to plan for | The host wishes to know who owns the best present | The invitation becomes invalid on the next day | The host must report the number to the school
exam: It is given that one is unable to attend a party. Select the response consistent with good manners. | "Thank you for inviting me, I'm sorry I can't come." | Say nothing at all and simply stay away on the day | "Your party sounds boring, so I'm not coming." | Ask whether the party may be moved for you
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the significance of a present? | The thought matters, not the price | The most expensive gift is the best gift | A present's price should be told to all | A present is worthless if it is homemade
exam: Identify the hazard most commonly associated with burst balloons at children's parties. | Young children may choke on the pieces | The pieces may set fire to the cake | The noise may damage the walls of the room | The rubber may stain the party clothes
